  "body": "Adds to https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/semantics.html#attr-html-manifest this caution:\r\n\r\n> The manifest-based application cache feature is in the process of being removed from the Web platform. (This is a long process that takes many years.) Using the application cache feature at this time is highly discouraged. Use service workers instead. [SW]\r\n\r\nhttps://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/browsers.html#offline has an existing caution which the above partially duplicates. The rationale for the partial redundancy is, we need to also have a caution at point of use where readers find the definition of the `manifest` attribute for the `html` element.\r\n\r\nPart of the context here is that I updated the HTML checker such that when it finds an `html` element with a `manifest` attribute, it emits the following warning:\r\n\r\n> [The manifest-based application cache feature is obsolete. Consider using service workers instead.](https://validator.w3.org/nu/?showsource=yes&doc=data%3Atext%2Fhtml%3Bcharset%3Dutf-8%2C%3C%2521DOCTYPE%2520html%3E%3Chtml%2520manifest%253Dfoo%3E%3Ctitle%3ETest%3C%252Ftitle%3E)\r\n\r\nBut there’s a potential problem with user confusion in that if today they go to part of the HTML spec  that defines `manifest`,  https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/semantics.html#attr-html-manifest, they won’t find any indication that they should avoid using `manifest`.\r\n\r\nSo among other things I’d like to avoid causing users who see that checker message to waste their time filing checker bugs saying, e.g., “The checker emits a warning that `manifest` shouldn’t be used, but the section of the HTML spec that talks about `manifest` doesn’t say it shouldn’t be used.”\r\n ",
